IRexp / IRSpectra-Bench — data licensing and attribution
=========================================================

This dataset redistributes ONLY extracted numeric data (IR band lists, 1H/13C shift
lists, and resolved structures as SMILES/SELFIES/InChIKey) together with each record's
source DOI/accession. No source full text, figures, or PDFs are redistributed.

Two source pools (Chemotion OK; PMC is a licence MIX — do not treat as all CC-BY)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. PMC Open-Access Subset  — 119,345 records — **MIXED Creative Commons terms**
   https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/tools/openftlist/
   PMC OA is partitioned into commercial-use (CC0 / CC BY / CC BY-SA / CC BY-ND),
   non-commercial (CC BY-NC*), and Other. A 2026-08-26 Europe PMC sample of 200
   IRexp PMCIDs found ~73% `cc by`, ~19% NC or empty, remainder other. **It is
   therefore incorrect to label the whole PMC pool CC-BY-4.0.** Per-article licence
   join and segregation (drop or NC-tag non-commercial) is required before any
   commercial-redistribution claim. Chemotion aside, released PMC rows currently
   carry `source_doi` only (no per-article licence stamp yet).
2. Chemotion RADAR4Chem FT-IR deposit — 1,888 records — CC-BY-SA-4.0
   DOI 10.22000/OGoEQGlsZGElrgst (resolves; landing page CC BY-SA 4.0)

The Chemotion vs PMC *source* pools are separable on `source_doi` (Chemotion uses
RADAR4Chem prefix 10.22000; PMC uses `PMC:` accessions):

    python scripts/split_license_pools.py --write OUT/

That script still stamps every non-Chemotion record as CC-BY-4.0 — **legacy
behaviour that overclaims**. Treat its PMC output as “PMC-sourced”, not as a
verified CC-BY pool, until article-level licences are joined. Any combined or
Chemotion-derived redistribution must honour CC-BY-SA-4.0 ShareAlike.

Attribution
-----------
Re-users must (a) cite the dataset via its archival Zenodo DOI (see PAPER.md
"Data and code availability"), and (b) attribute the originating publications via each
record's `source_doi` field, under each article's actual licence terms.

